import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="TB-500: Unapproved Research Chemical — Nawtch"
  description="TB-500 has no FDA approval and zero completed human trials — what's actually known, its legal status, and why caution matters."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/peptides/tb-500/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
      <header>
        <div class="header-left">
          <h1>TB-500</h1>
          <p class="subtitle">No approval, no completed human trials — sold explicitly as a research-only chemical.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/peptides/">← Back to Peptides</a>
      </header>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent); background:rgba(200,240,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent);">Last Verified: August 14, 2026</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--warn); background:rgba(240,96,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--danger);">Tier 3 — Unapproved Research Chemical</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            TB-500 is sold online labeled "not for human consumption," specifically to avoid FDA
            drug regulation. <strong>There is little to no human safety data.</strong> This page
            describes what's known — <strong>not dosing or usage guidance, and not a
            recommendation to use it.</strong> Talk to a doctor before considering it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            TB-500 is a synthetic fragment/analog of thymosin beta-4, a protein naturally involved
            in cell structure and migration. Its proposed mechanism, based entirely on animal and
            cell-culture research, involves regulating actin (a structural protein) to promote
            cell movement and tissue repair — the basis for its marketing around injury recovery.
            For the native, full-length 43-amino-acid protein this fragment is derived from —
            which has its own, more substantial human trial history — see our separate <a href="/supplements/peptides/thymosin-beta-4/">Thymosin Beta-4</a> page.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Research Actually Exists</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            There are no completed human clinical trials of TB-500. What exists is preclinical —
            animal and cell studies looking at wound healing and tissue repair. There is
            essentially no human evidence for safety or effectiveness, which is different from
            "the human evidence is weak" — for TB-500 specifically, it doesn't meaningfully exist
            yet.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Current Legal &amp; Regulatory Status</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            TB-500 has never been FDA-approved for any use. It is sold as a "research chemical,"
            explicitly labeled not for human consumption — language sellers use specifically to
            operate outside FDA drug regulation. Its compounding-pharmacy status has shifted more
            than once in 2026: previously restricted from compounding (the FDA's "Category 2"
            list), that restriction was lifted effective April 22, 2026 when the nomination was
            withdrawn, and on July 23, 2026 the FDA's Pharmacy Compounding Advisory Committee
            voted 8-6 (with one abstention) to recommend adding it to the compounding-permitted
            list. That's a recommendation, not a rule change — compounding pharmacies cannot
            legally use TB-500 under Section 503A until the FDA completes formal rulemaking, which
            typically takes 8–24 months. None of this amounts to drug approval; it only affects
            whether a compounding pharmacy may eventually be allowed to prepare it. See our <a href="/supplements/peptides/">Peptides hub page</a> for the fuller regulatory picture across this
            group of compounds. It's also prohibited at all times in competitive sport under the
            World Anti-Doping Agency's Prohibited List.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding/bulk-drug-substances-used-compounding-under-section-503a-fdc-act" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA — Bulk Drug Substances Used in Compounding →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Known &amp; Theoretical Risks</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            With no completed human trials, real risks in people — immune reactions, impurity
            from unregulated manufacturing, long-term effects — are unstudied rather than ruled
            out. Products sold as "TB-500" also carry the additional, practical risk of unverified
            purity and dosing consistency, since they aren't manufactured under pharmaceutical
            quality standards.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding/bulk-drug-substances-used-compounding-under-section-503a-fdc-act" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA — Bulk Drug Substances Used in Compounding Under Section 503A</a>
        </div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
